WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:03.740
In this section, we're going to just briefly cover

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.620
a few different features and tools that

3
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.859
are available to you that really tie intrinsically

4
00:00:09.859 --> 00:00:12.099
to Sequencer, building out these shots,

5
00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:14.259
or creating these really beautiful cinematic

6
00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:15.220
experiences.

7
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.559
So one of the things that, and this has

8
00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:20.120
been in the engine for quite a while, is Take Recorder.

9
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.839
Now, Take Recorder allows you to control actors or record

10
00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:26.440
motion for them.

11
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.839
As an example, so here we have two meerkats on screen,

12
00:00:29.879 --> 00:00:32.679
and you'll see inside of Take Recorder

13
00:00:32.679 --> 00:00:35.659
that we have both of these actors present.

14
00:00:35.659 --> 00:00:37.280
So let's say that we're creating this,

15
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.159
I don't know, a dialogue between the two meerkats.

16
00:00:39.159 --> 00:00:40.039
They're talking to each other.

17
00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:40.960
They're moving.

18
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.000
And I'm using a motion capture suit to control them,

19
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.880
basically puppet them in engine.

20
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.840
But it's only me, lonely me, doing this recording.

21
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.960
So how can I do both actors without having to have two suits?

22
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.359
We use a Take Recorder for that.

23
00:00:55.359 --> 00:00:57.240
So with this little toggle here,

24
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.399
I can turn on and off recording that particular actor.

25
00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:05.040
So I could have the first meerkat turned on,

26
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.840
hit my record button, it will do a countdown,

27
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.760
and then it will start recording to a sequence

28
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.040
all of the animations that it's recording

29
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.120
from my motion capture.

30
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.000
I can hit stop.

31
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.920
It'll record all that information.

32
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.959
I will then deactivate the first one,

33
00:01:18.959 --> 00:01:22.200
which I was just controlling, activate the second one,

34
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.180
hit my Take Record.

35
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.840
It will go ahead and automatically advance to another.

36
00:01:25.879 --> 00:01:27.920
I'll record the secondary meerkat,

37
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.579
and then I can simply squish all these together

38
00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:32.560
in a separate sequence.

39
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.000
And now I have both actors

40
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.799
interacting and working together.

41
00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:37.120
So again, that's just one example of it,

42
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.659
but Take Recorder is just a great way

43
00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:41.560
to rapidly record information

44
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.959
from some kind of live source,

45
00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:47.280
whether that's a virtual camera, controlling actors,

46
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.640
even using controller input as well.

47
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.920
All of those things can be controlled

48
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.780
and recorded directly through Take Recorder.

49
00:01:57.299 --> 00:01:58.620
We talked about this in one of the previous videos

50
00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.379
about Live Link Face, as well as MetaHuman Animator.

51
00:02:02.379 --> 00:02:04.400
This is a phenomenal tool.

52
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.180
It essentially allows you to take an iOS device

53
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.860
and record basically anything,

54
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.020
whether it's your facial animation

55
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.659
or potentially moving your body

56
00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:15.539
or using it as a camera.

57
00:02:15.539 --> 00:02:18.020
All of this is completely markerless,

58
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:21.349
meaning that you can, just as an example here,

59
00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:23.090
point the iOS device at your face,

60
00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.710
and you can instantly start recording facial animations

61
00:02:27.229 --> 00:02:28.069
in the editor.

62
00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:31.169
Now this one does require Live Link Face turned on

63
00:02:31.169 --> 00:02:32.870
and the app installed on your device.

64
00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.110
But again, these are 100% completely free

65
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.569
and you can do that.

66
00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:38.449
We also talked too about our VCAM,

67
00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:41.550
that we can use the same crosstalk interface

68
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.069
behind the scenes, Live Link,

69
00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:46.509
to control a virtual camera within

70
00:02:46.509 --> 00:02:47.909
the actual editor itself.

71
00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:50.889
And of course, record that information to a sequence.

72
00:02:50.889 --> 00:02:52.870
So we can do that through Live Link VCAM,

73
00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.550
Unreal Remote, both of these are completely free.

74
00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.030
Now, if you're watching this video,

75
00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.229
I do recommend using the hot pink one,

76
00:03:00.229 --> 00:03:01.990
which is the Live Link VCAM,

77
00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.509
will allow you to connect through that.

78
00:03:03.509 --> 00:03:06.759
That is the more up to date one, but I digress.

79
00:03:06.759 --> 00:03:10.159
So with it too, one of the pro tips I recommend is

80
00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:13.759
if you are considering using a virtual camera

81
00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:16.340
with a iOS device,

82
00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.319
a very handy thing to get you more realistic

83
00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:22.039
or more natural feeling camera work is,

84
00:03:22.039 --> 00:03:24.780
you know, the iPad by itself is a fairly light device.

85
00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.580
And so it's gonna translate very subtle movements

86
00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.240
of your hand or your body moving a lot more

87
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.610
than if it had some weight.

88
00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.530
There's various different companies out there,

89
00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.729
not gonna plug any of them,

90
00:03:35.729 --> 00:03:38.669
but you can find essentially what are shoulder rigs

91
00:03:38.669 --> 00:03:41.889
or camera rigs that will hold an iPad for you,

92
00:03:41.889 --> 00:03:44.210
allowing you to put like extra weight on it,

93
00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.530
to put it on your shoulder, to handle it.

94
00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:50.050
That small addition to your workflow

95
00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:52.949
is going to have very, very big implications

96
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.909
and a very big impact on the final look

97
00:03:55.909 --> 00:03:57.870
of that camera movement.

98
00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.710
So I highly recommend if you're gonna be using

99
00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:01.830
a virtual camera, definitely get some different rigs

100
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.870
to hold the iPad, to add some weight,

101
00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.310
and that will just make things feel more natural

102
00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.789
to the user and hopefully require less

103
00:04:09.789 --> 00:04:13.990
kind of post editing with the different key frames.

104
00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:15.110
Another fun note, pro tip here,

105
00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:18.149
is that you can actually use a Nintendo Switch controller

106
00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:21.370
with iOS 16 or later, which is really great

107
00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:23.350
because you can actually map these camera controls

108
00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.709
like zooming in, zooming out, your f-stop,

109
00:04:26.709 --> 00:04:29.230
translation, et cetera, extremely handy

110
00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.310
and extremely quick, especially when working

111
00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.389
with take recorder to quickly do some camera movements,

112
00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:37.990
reshoot it with almost little to no downtime.

113
00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:39.949
And then as a note too,

114
00:04:39.949 --> 00:04:42.829
without shamelessly plugging iOS devices,

115
00:04:42.829 --> 00:04:45.750
I will say this, that they are more widely supported

116
00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.029
and that is a testament to Apple's hardware

117
00:04:48.029 --> 00:04:51.050
in that it's just the forward facing cameras,

118
00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.290
the depth sensors, the gyroscopes as well,

119
00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:56.769
are very, very high quality and it's one of the reasons

120
00:04:56.769 --> 00:05:00.889
why you'll find like the V cam as well as Live Link,

121
00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:02.949
why they just work a little bit better with Apple.

122
00:05:02.949 --> 00:05:05.889
However, there is an Android device out there,

123
00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:08.410
but my recommendation is if you're gonna be using

124
00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:11.129
this workflow with a mobile device,

125
00:05:11.129 --> 00:05:12.889
again, if you're capturing your face

126
00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:14.629
or you're capturing a V cam,

127
00:05:14.629 --> 00:05:16.649
I would highly recommend using an Apple device

128
00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.949
over an Android, just because again,

129
00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.029
you'll get a higher quality output

130
00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:23.189
and there's more supported features,

131
00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:26.370
but just know that the virtual app by Owl Studio

132
00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:30.620
does work with Live Link with a V cam, so it's there,

133
00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.279
but it is also not a free plugin anymore,

134
00:05:33.279 --> 00:05:36.310
it is now $80, so keep that in mind.

135
00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.250
Another really great tool is Epos,

136
00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.529
which actually comes from Praxinos,

137
00:05:40.529 --> 00:05:42.970
they've got the Epos in Iliad as well.

138
00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.970
And this is a great tool that allows you

139
00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.949
to essentially do storyboarding directly

140
00:05:49.829 --> 00:05:52.189
within Sequencer, in fact, you can create this,

141
00:05:52.189 --> 00:05:54.949
I'll just say piece of content for lack of better terms,

142
00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:57.589
the container for Epos, and it'll actually allow you

143
00:05:57.589 --> 00:06:00.389
to storyboard on the actual sequence itself,

144
00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:03.350
see playbacks, this is amazing for creating animatics

145
00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:07.629
or even doing some editing, you can even do blending

146
00:06:07.629 --> 00:06:11.709
of 2D, so what you're actually gonna be doing in Epos,

147
00:06:11.709 --> 00:06:15.089
some of these 2D elements with the actual scene itself,

148
00:06:15.089 --> 00:06:18.370
which is just great, so this is a phenomenal tool to use,

149
00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.810
it is free to use, it's on the Unreal Engine Marketplace,

150
00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:25.000
again, Epos and Iliad are the two tools for that.

151
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
And then here's Iliad as well, which again,

152
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.579
allows you to do some traditional texture painting,

153
00:06:29.579 --> 00:06:31.639
great resources from them.
